messages.content:
Form 17A, cited in rules 56A and 145AA, is a "Receipt for samples of drugs or cosmetics taken where fair price tendered thereof under sub‑section (I) of Section 23 of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 is refused." This form is required whenever a regulatory officer collects a sample but the person from whom the sample is taken declines to pay the fair market price that the Act mandates for such samples. The form records: 
- The name of the person or entity from whom the sample was taken; 
- The date of the collection; 
- The name of the officer taking the sample; 
- The address of the premises; and 
- A statement that the fair‑price tender was refused, referencing "sub‑section (I) of Section 23 of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940." Thus, Form 17A serves as documentary evidence of the refusal and the legal basis for the officer's right to retain the sample without payment.

Form 17, referenced in rules 56 and 145A of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules 1945, is an "Intimation to person from whom sample is taken." Its purpose is to formally notify the owner or occupier of a premises that a sample of drugs or cosmetics has been collected for testing or analysis. According to the text, the form must contain: 
- The name and address of the premises from which the sample was taken ("premises of ………………………….situated at............................................");
- A description of the drugs/cosmetics sampled ("samples of the drugs / cosmetics specified below");
- The date of collection; 
- The name and signature of the inspecting officer ("Inspector. …………………….");
- A section for "Details of samples taken" where the specific items, quantities, and any batch numbers are recorded. All these elements ensure a clear audit trail for regulatory compliance.
